course: dictionary
lesson: 4685
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words mean different things in different parts of the world. Homely is one of them. In British and Australian English, «a homely kitchen» is a kitchen that feels cosy, warm and welcoming, like a place where you want to sit down with a cup of tea.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. If the sentence talks about a warm room, a simple meal or a friendly welcome, homely means cosy. If the sentence talks about how someone looks, the writer may be using American English, where homely means plain and not good-looking.
explain: Check where the writer comes from. A story set in London or Sydney will usually use the British or Australian meaning, while an American writer may use the other one. When you are not sure, ask: does this sentence describe a place, or a person's face?
fact: Homely means simple, warm and comfortable, like a cosy home (UK and Australia).
fact: «A homely kitchen» means a simple, warm kitchen where people feel at ease.
fact: In American and Canadian English, homely means plain and not good-looking.
fact: Calling a person homely in American English is unkind, so be careful with it.
fact: Homely changes form like this: homely, homelier, homeliest.
exercise: «The homely kitchen smelled of warm bread and soup.» What does homely mean here? | Very large and grand | Cold and empty | * Cosy and welcoming, like home | Plain and not good-looking
exercise: An American writer says, «She is quite homely.» What does homely mean in American English? | Very clever | Happy and bright | Cosy and friendly | * Plain, not very pretty
exercise: Find and copy one word from «The homely kitchen smelled of warm bread and soup.» that is a clue that the kitchen is cosy. | soup | kitchen | smelled | * warm
test: «We sat by the fire in a homely little café.» In British English, what does homely mean? | Noisy and crowded | * Simple, warm and comfortable | Plain and not good-looking | Expensive and grand
test: A friend from Canada says, «Our hotel room felt homely.» What is the clue that tells you which meaning to use? | The word friend means the meaning is unkind | * The writer is Canadian, so homely may mean plain, but the word felt makes it sound like a place, so check the whole sentence | The word room means it is always cosy | The word hotel means it is always plain
exam: «The homely cottage had a warm kitchen and a crackling fire.» In British English, what does homely mean? | * Cosy and welcoming | Old and falling down | Plain and not good-looking | Rich and expensive
